# Cardiac Pathology (Gardner) — Version 1, 15 items (6 with images)
Q = [
dict(img=None,
stem="A 66-year-old man comes to the office because of progressive swelling of his ankles and a feeling of abdominal fullness for 4 months. He has severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and has smoked 2 packs of cigarettes daily for 45 years. He has no history of myocardial infarction or valve disease. His pulse is 96/min and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 86%. Jugular venous pressure is elevated, the pulmonic component of the second heart sound is loud, and a parasternal heave is felt. Diffuse expiratory wheezes are heard. The liver is enlarged and tender, and there is 2+ pitting edema of both legs. Serum albumin concentration is 4.1 g/dL. Echocardiography shows a thickened, dilated right ventricle, a normal-sized left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 60%, and no intracardiac shunt.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this patient's edema?",
choices=["Back-pressure from a failing left ventricle","Hypoxic constriction of pulmonary arterioles","Left-to-right shunting through a septal defect","Low plasma oncotic pressure from malnutrition","Obstruction of hepatic venous outflow"], key=1,
exp="Right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (raised neck veins, loud pulmonic second sound, parasternal heave, tender liver, leg edema) caused by lung disease, with a normal left ventricle, is <b>cor pulmonale</b>.<br><br>Severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease → alveolar hypoxia → <b>hypoxic vasoconstriction of pulmonary arterioles</b> (plus loss of capillary bed) → raised pulmonary vascular resistance → right ventricular pressure overload → right ventricular hypertrophy, then dilation and failure → raised systemic venous pressure → edema and congestive hepatomegaly. The lecture's point: most right heart failure is secondary to left heart failure, and cor pulmonale is the rest, where the cause is in the lungs or pulmonary vessels.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Cor pulmonale is right ventricular hypertrophy and failure from raised pulmonary vascular resistance due to lung disease (for example hypoxic vasoconstriction in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease), with a normal left ventricle.",
wrong={0:"Left heart failure is the commonest cause of right heart failure, but his left ventricle is normal-sized with an ejection fraction of 60%.",
2:"A left-to-right shunt can overload the right heart and pulmonary circulation, but echocardiography shows no shunt.",
3:"Low oncotic pressure causes edema without distended neck veins, and his albumin is normal (4.1 g/dL).",
4:"Hepatic venous obstruction (Budd–Chiari syndrome) causes tender hepatomegaly and ascites, but not raised jugular venous pressure or right ventricular hypertrophy."},
eli5="His damaged lungs can't get enough oxygen in, so the blood vessels in the lungs clamp down. The right side of the heart has to push against that squeeze and eventually wears out. Blood then backs up into his liver and legs."),

dict(img='eisen', cap='Heart at autopsy, right side opened; the examiner\'s fingers hold the right ventricular wall',
stem="A 34-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of worsening shortness of breath for 1 year. As a child she was told she had a heart murmur, but she never had follow-up. Over the past 6 months, her lips have turned blue with exertion. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 83%. There is clubbing of the fingers and toes. The second heart sound is loud and single, and no murmur is heard. Hematocrit is 61%. Despite treatment, she dies of a massive hemoptysis 2 weeks later. A photograph of the heart at autopsy is shown; the right ventricular wall is markedly thickened.{FIG}Which of the following best explains why this patient's cyanosis appeared in adulthood?",
choices=["Acquired narrowing of the right ventricular outflow","Left ventricular failure raising left atrial pressure","Pulmonary vascular remodeling reversing shunt flow","Recurrent pneumonia impairing alveolar gas exchange","Spontaneous closure of the defect by fibrous tissue"], key=2,
exp="The photograph shows a large <b>atrial septal defect</b> (diamond) and a thick-walled right ventricle.<br><br>For decades the defect shunted <b>left-to-right</b> (acyanotic) → excess pulmonary blood flow → injury to the pulmonary arterioles → medial hypertrophy and intimal fibrosis → <b>irreversible pulmonary hypertension</b> → right-sided pressures rise above left-sided pressures → the shunt <b>reverses to right-to-left</b> → late cyanosis, clubbing and polycythemia (hematocrit 61%). This is <b>Eisenmenger syndrome</b>, which can follow any long-standing left-to-right shunt (ventricular septal defect, atrial septal defect, patent ductus arteriosus). The murmur disappears as the pressures equalize. Atrial septal defects are often diagnosed late, which is why this happens in adults.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Eisenmenger syndrome is late cyanosis caused by pulmonary vascular remodeling and pulmonary hypertension that reverse a long-standing left-to-right shunt.",
wrong={0:"Right ventricular outflow narrowing with a septal defect is the mechanism of <b>tetralogy of Fallot</b>, which causes cyanosis from infancy, not a childhood murmur followed by cyanosis decades later.",
1:"A rise in left atrial pressure would push <b>more</b> blood left-to-right across an atrial defect, which would not cause cyanosis.",
3:"Lung disease can cause hypoxemia, but it would not explain the atrial septal defect, the loss of the childhood murmur, or right-to-left flow of this degree.",
4:"If the defect had closed, the shunt would stop and there would be no cyanosis. The photograph shows the defect still widely open."},
eli5="For years a hole between her heart's upper chambers sent extra blood to her lungs. The lung vessels got damaged and stiff, and pressure on the right side climbed higher than the left. Then blood started flowing the wrong way through the hole, and blue blood reached her body."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A newborn boy delivered at 39 weeks' gestation becomes increasingly cyanotic on day 2 of life. His pulse is 164/min and respirations are 62/min. Pulse oximetry shows an oxygen saturation of 62% that does not improve with 100% oxygen. No murmur is heard. Chest x-ray shows a narrow mediastinum with an egg-shaped cardiac silhouette. Echocardiography shows that the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery arises from the left ventricle. The ventricular septum is intact, and a small, narrowing ductus arteriosus is present.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate pharmacotherapy?",
choices=["Alprostadil","Furosemide","Indomethacin","Inhaled nitric oxide","Propranolol"], key=0,
exp="The aorta arising from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle is <b>transposition of the great arteries</b>. It creates two <b>parallel</b> circuits: deoxygenated blood loops through the body and oxygenated blood loops through the lungs.<br><br>This is incompatible with life unless the circuits can <b>mix</b>. A ventricular septal defect gives stable mixing. With an intact septum, the only mixing site is the <b>ductus arteriosus</b> (unstable) and the foramen ovale → the ductus closes on day 1–3 → profound cyanosis. <b>Prostaglandin E1 (alprostadil)</b> keeps the ductus open until a balloon atrial septostomy and then the arterial switch operation.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In transposition of the great arteries with an intact ventricular septum, give prostaglandin E1 to keep the ductus arteriosus open so the two parallel circulations can mix.",
wrong={1:"Furosemide treats volume overload, as in a large left-to-right shunt. It does nothing for a lack of mixing between parallel circuits.",
2:"Indomethacin <b>closes</b> the ductus, which would remove the only mixing site and could kill this infant.",
3:"Inhaled nitric oxide lowers pulmonary vascular resistance in persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn. There the heart is structurally normal, so it cannot fix transposed vessels.",
4:"Propranolol is used for hypercyanotic \"tet spells\" in tetralogy of Fallot. It does not create mixing in transposition."},
eli5="His two main arteries are hooked up to the wrong pumping chambers, so the oxygen-rich blood never gets out to his body. A small fetal blood vessel was letting the two streams mix, but it's closing. A prostaglandin medicine keeps that vessel open until surgery."),

dict(img='pda', cap='Great vessels at autopsy (arrow marks the abnormality)',
stem="A boy born at 27 weeks' gestation has a continuous, machine-like murmur heard best below the left clavicle on day 5 of life. His peripheral pulses are bounding, and blood pressure is 58/22 mm Hg. He requires increasing ventilator support. Treatment for the murmur was deferred because of thrombocytopenia. He dies at 3 weeks of age of necrotizing enterocolitis. The photograph obtained at autopsy is shown.{FIG}Administration of which of the following drugs would most likely have closed the structure indicated by the arrow?",
choices=["Alprostadil","Digoxin","Indomethacin","Inhaled nitric oxide","Sildenafil"], key=2,
exp="The arrow marks a <b>patent ductus arteriosus</b> joining the aorta and the pulmonary artery. The clues are prematurity, a continuous machine-like murmur below the left clavicle, bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure (58/22 mm Hg). Aortic blood runs off into the pulmonary artery through systole and diastole → a left-to-right shunt → excess pulmonary flow → eventually pulmonary hypertension.<br><br>In the fetus, low oxygen tension plus <b>prostaglandin E2</b> keep the ductus open. Premature infants have immature ductal smooth muscle and high circulating prostaglandins. A <b>cyclooxygenase inhibitor (indomethacin or ibuprofen)</b> → less prostaglandin E2 → ductal constriction and closure. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs impair platelet function, which is why treatment was held for thrombocytopenia.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A patent ductus arteriosus is kept open by prostaglandin E2 and closed with a cyclooxygenase inhibitor such as indomethacin. Alprostadil (prostaglandin E1) keeps it open.",
wrong={0:"Alprostadil (prostaglandin E1) keeps the ductus <b>open</b>. It is used in ductus-dependent lesions such as transposition of the great arteries.",
1:"Digoxin increases contractility in heart failure but does not affect ductal smooth muscle tone.",
3:"Inhaled nitric oxide is a pulmonary vasodilator used for persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn. It does not constrict the ductus.",
4:"Sildenafil, a phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor, also dilates the pulmonary vessels. It would not close the ductus."},
eli5="Before birth, a short blood vessel lets blood skip the lungs, and a chemical called prostaglandin keeps it open. In tiny premature babies it often stays open after birth. A medicine like indomethacin blocks prostaglandin, which helps the vessel squeeze shut."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 16-year-old boy comes to the office because of headaches and leg cramps with running for 6 months. His blood pressure is 164/88 mm Hg in the right arm and 104/68 mm Hg in the right leg. The femoral pulses are weak and delayed compared with the radial pulses. A systolic murmur is heard over the left side of the back between the shoulder blades. He has no skin lesions. Chest x-ray shows notching of the inferior borders of the posterior ribs.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the rib findings on this patient's x-ray?",
choices=["Dilated collateral veins from vena cava obstruction","Enlarged intercostal arteries eroding the ribs","Neurofibromas growing along intercostal nerves","Periosteal new bone from chronic hypoxemia","Resorption of bone from excess parathyroid hormone"], key=1,
exp="High blood pressure in the arms with low, delayed pulses in the legs, claudication and an interscapular murmur is <b>coarctation of the aorta</b>. This is a focal narrowing of the aorta, usually just beyond the left subclavian artery near the ductus.<br><br>Obstruction → blood detours around the narrowing: subclavian artery → internal thoracic artery → <b>intercostal arteries</b> → descending aorta. Over years these collateral arteries enlarge, become tortuous and pulsatile → they <b>erode the undersurface of the ribs</b> → rib notching. Coarctation accounts for about 5% of congenital heart disease and is acyanotic. It is associated with Turner syndrome and a bicuspid aortic valve, and previous coarctation repair is a risk factor for endocarditis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Rib notching in coarctation of the aorta is caused by enlarged intercostal collateral arteries eroding the ribs.",
wrong={0:"Superior vena cava obstruction dilates <b>veins</b> over the chest wall and swells the face and arms. It does not cause a blood pressure gap between arms and legs.",
2:"Neurofibromatosis type 1 can cause rib erosion from neurofibromas (and renal artery stenosis), but he has no skin lesions such as café-au-lait spots.",
3:"Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy (periosteal new bone with clubbing) follows lung cancer or chronic hypoxemia. It thickens long bones rather than notching ribs.",
4:"Hyperparathyroidism causes subperiosteal resorption, typically of the phalanges, not notching of the undersides of the ribs, and it does not explain the arm–leg blood pressure difference."},
eli5="His main artery is pinched just after the arm branches, so the arms get high pressure and the legs get low pressure. Blood finds a detour through little arteries under the ribs. Those arteries grow big and wear grooves into the ribs."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A newborn girl has a seizure on day 3 of life. She has a small jaw, low-set ears, and a cleft palate. A loud systolic murmur is heard. Serum calcium concentration is 6.2 mg/dL. Chest x-ray shows no thymic shadow. Echocardiography shows a single great artery arising from both ventricles above a ventricular septal defect.{FIG}A defect in which of the following genes is most likely responsible for this patient's condition?",
choices=["JAG1","NKX2.5","PTPN11","TBX1","TBX5"], key=3,
exp="Cardiac outflow-tract defect (a single great artery, <b>truncus arteriosus</b>) + hypocalcemic seizure (no parathyroids) + absent thymus + cleft palate and abnormal face is <b>DiGeorge syndrome</b>. It is a <b>22q11.2 deletion</b> that removes <b>TBX1</b>, a transcription factor.<br><br>Failure of the 3rd and 4th pharyngeal pouches and of the neural crest-derived outflow septum → thymic aplasia (T-cell deficiency), parathyroid aplasia (hypocalcemia), and conotruncal defects (truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, interrupted aortic arch). The lecture's point: ~90% of congenital heart defects are unexplained, and the known genes are mostly transcription factors that change the timing of heart development.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> DiGeorge syndrome (22q11 deletion, TBX1) combines conotruncal heart defects with thymic and parathyroid aplasia.",
wrong={0:"JAG1 (NOTCH signalling) mutations cause <b>Alagille syndrome</b>: pulmonary artery stenosis or tetralogy of Fallot with bile-duct paucity and butterfly vertebrae, not hypocalcemia or an absent thymus.",
1:"NKX2.5 mutations cause <b>nonsyndromic</b> atrial septal defects and conduction defects without facial, thymic or parathyroid involvement.",
2:"PTPN11 mutations cause <b>Noonan syndrome</b>: pulmonary valve stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, webbed neck and short stature.",
4:"TBX5 mutations cause <b>Holt–Oram syndrome</b>: atrial and ventricular septal defects and conduction defects with upper-limb (thumb and radius) malformations."},
eli5="A tiny missing piece of chromosome 22 disrupts how the face, thymus, calcium glands and heart outlet form in the womb. So she has one big artery instead of two, low calcium that caused a seizure, and no thymus. The missing gene is called TBX1."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 68-year-old man is admitted to the hospital with an acute inferior wall myocardial infarction and is treated medically. On hospital day 4, he suddenly becomes severely short of breath. His pulse is 124/min, respirations are 32/min, and blood pressure is 88/54 mm Hg. Crackles are heard throughout both lung fields, and there is a new soft holosystolic murmur at the apex. Jugular venous pressure is normal. Right heart catheterization shows no increase in oxygen saturation between the right atrium and the right ventricle, and the pulmonary capillary wedge tracing shows large v waves. There is no pericardial effusion on bedside ultrasonography.{FIG}Rupture of which of the following structures most likely caused this patient's deterioration?",
choices=["Anterolateral papillary muscle","Interventricular septum","Left ventricular free wall","Posteromedial papillary muscle","Right ventricular free wall"], key=3,
exp="Sudden pulmonary edema and hypotension with a new apical holosystolic murmur and <b>large v waves</b> on the wedge tracing (the left atrium is being filled by a regurgitant jet) = <b>acute mitral regurgitation</b> from papillary muscle rupture. It typically happens at <b>3–7 days</b>, when the infarct is softest.<br><br>Which papillary muscle: the <b>posteromedial</b> papillary muscle usually has a <b>single</b> blood supply (the posterior descending artery, usually from the right coronary artery), so an inferior infarct can kill it. The anterolateral papillary muscle has a dual supply (left anterior descending and circumflex arteries) and rarely ruptures. The lecture flags papillary rupture as a volume load on an already weak heart and a common cause of death.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Acute mitral regurgitation 3–7 days after an inferior myocardial infarction is posteromedial papillary muscle rupture. That muscle has a single blood supply from the posterior descending artery.",
wrong={0:"The anterolateral papillary muscle has a dual blood supply (left anterior descending and left circumflex) and rarely ruptures, least of all after an inferior infarct.",
1:"Septal rupture also causes a new holosystolic murmur in the same window, but it produces an <b>oxygen step-up</b> between the right atrium and right ventricle. None is present.",
2:"Free wall rupture causes hemopericardium and tamponade (distended neck veins, pulseless electrical activity), not a new murmur with pulmonary edema. There is no effusion.",
4:"A right ventricular infarct or rupture causes hypotension with <b>raised</b> neck veins and clear lungs, which is the opposite of this picture."},
eli5="The heart attack killed one of the little muscles that hold the mitral valve's flaps shut. A few days later that muscle snapped, the valve flopped open, and blood gushed backward into the lungs. The bottom-back muscle is the vulnerable one because it has only one artery feeding it."),

dict(img='cihd', cap='Cross-section of the ventricles at autopsy',
stem="A 69-year-old woman with a 25-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus complicated by peripheral neuropathy has had progressive shortness of breath and leg swelling for 3 years. She has never reported chest pain. She does not drink alcohol and has never received chemotherapy. She dies at home. At autopsy, a cross-section of the heart is shown. The three major coronary arteries each show 80–90% narrowing by atherosclerotic plaque without fresh thrombus. Microscopic examination shows scattered, patchy areas of dense collagen replacing myocytes; there is no inflammation, granulomas, or amyloid.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Cardiac sarcoidosis","Chronic ischemic heart disease","Hypertensive heart disease","Idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy","Viral myocarditis"], key=1,
exp="The left ventricle is dilated with a thinned wall and white scarring (diamond), severe three-vessel coronary disease, and patchy dense scars without an acute infarct. This is <b>chronic ischemic heart disease</b> (ischemic cardiomyopathy).<br><br>Severe coronary narrowing → repeated small infarcts → progressive <b>myocyte drop-out</b> replaced by patchy scar → dilation and systolic failure. The lecture stresses that this often happens <b>silently</b> in diabetics, because autonomic neuropathy blunts the visceral pain of ischemia. She never had chest pain.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Progressive heart failure with a dilated ventricle, patchy scars and severe multivessel coronary disease is chronic ischemic heart disease, often from silent infarcts in diabetics.",
wrong={0:"Cardiac sarcoidosis would show non-caseating <b>granulomas</b>, often with heart block. None are present.",
2:"Hypertensive heart disease causes <b>concentric</b> hypertrophy (a thick wall, small cavity), not a thin dilated ventricle with patchy ischemic scars.",
3:"Idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy looks similar grossly, but it is a diagnosis of exclusion. It is ruled out when severe coronary disease explains the scarring (\"ischemic heart disease\" is the lecture's classic look-alike).",
4:"Viral myocarditis would show lymphocytic inflammation with myocyte injury, not dense collagen scars behind 80–90% coronary stenoses."},
eli5="Her heart arteries were badly clogged, and over the years many small patches of heart muscle died and turned into scar. Because diabetes had damaged her nerves, she never felt the chest pain that normally warns of a heart attack. The scarred heart stretched out and got weak."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 58-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of sharp chest pain and fever for 2 days. Three weeks ago, he had an anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction treated with a stent in the left anterior descending artery, and he was discharged on day 4. The pain now worsens with deep breathing and improves when he sits forward. His temperature is 38.3°C (100.9°F), pulse is 98/min, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. A scratchy, three-component sound is heard at the left sternal border. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 64 mm/h. Serum troponin I concentration is lower than at discharge. The catheter access site is clean. Echocardiography shows a small pericardial effusion and no new wall-motion abnormality.{FIG}Which of the following best explains this patient's current condition?",
choices=["Autoimmune reaction to exposed myocardial antigens","Bacterial infection introduced at catheterization","Inflammation spreading from fresh necrosis","Reinfarction from thrombosis within the stent","Viral infection of the pericardial lining"], key=0,
exp="Fever, pleuritic positional chest pain, a friction rub, a raised sedimentation rate and a small effusion <b>weeks</b> after a myocardial infarction is <b>Dressler syndrome</b> (post-myocardial infarction syndrome).<br><br>Myocyte necrosis → cardiac antigens previously hidden from the immune system are exposed → antibodies form over <b>1–2+ weeks</b> → immune-mediated (autoimmune) pericarditis. The lecture contrasts two post-infarct pericarditides: <b>early (2–3 days)</b>, fibrinous inflammation spreading directly from a transmural infarct, and <b>Dressler (more than 2 weeks)</b>, autoimmune. The same mechanism causes post-pericardiotomy syndrome after cardiac surgery.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Pericarditis more than 2 weeks after a myocardial infarction is Dressler syndrome, an autoimmune reaction to newly exposed myocardial antigens.",
wrong={1:"Bacterial (purulent) pericarditis would make him far sicker, with high fevers, often tamponade, and a source. The access site is clean.",
2:"Direct spread from fresh transmural necrosis causes fibrinous pericarditis in the first <b>2–3 days</b>. He is 3 weeks out.",
3:"Stent thrombosis would cause a new ischemic injury, with rising troponin and a new wall-motion abnormality. His troponin is falling and wall motion is unchanged.",
4:"Viral pericarditis looks similar clinically, but the tight timing after the infarct points to Dressler syndrome, and no viral prodrome is described."},
eli5="The heart attack broke open heart cells and showed the immune system proteins it had never seen. A few weeks later the body made antibodies against them. Those antibodies attacked the sac around the heart, causing fever and a painful rub."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 52-year-old man comes to the office because of chest tightness and lightheadedness while climbing stairs for 3 months. Last week he fainted while running for a bus. He has no history of rheumatic fever. His pulse is 72/min and blood pressure is 118/94 mm Hg. A harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur is heard at the right upper sternal border, radiating to both carotid arteries. The second heart sound is soft and single. The carotid upstrokes are weak and delayed. Echocardiography shows a heavily calcified aortic valve with a severely reduced opening; the mitral valve is normal, and the interventricular septum is of normal thickness.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's condition?",
choices=["Age-related calcification of a tricuspid valve","Calcification of a congenitally bicuspid valve","Chronic rheumatic scarring of the valve","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy","Syphilitic inflammation of the aortic root"], key=1,
exp="Exertional angina, syncope and dyspnea, a crescendo-decrescendo murmur radiating to the carotids, a soft single second sound, and slow weak carotid upstrokes (pulsus parvus et tardus) = <b>severe calcific aortic stenosis</b>.<br><br>The age is the clue. The lecture's causes of aortic stenosis are age-related calcification, calcification of a <b>congenitally bicuspid</b> valve, and rheumatic scarring. A bicuspid valve (about 1–2% of people) is exposed to abnormal mechanical stress → it calcifies and stenoses <b>decades earlier</b> (typically 40s–50s) than a normal tricuspid valve (typically after 70).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Severe calcific aortic stenosis in a patient in his 50s most likely comes from a congenitally bicuspid aortic valve, which calcifies decades earlier than a normal tricuspid valve.",
wrong={0:"Senile calcific stenosis of a normal tricuspid valve is the commonest cause overall, but it typically presents after age 70, not at 52.",
2:"Rheumatic aortic stenosis almost always comes with <b>mitral</b> involvement (commissural fusion), and his mitral valve is normal with no rheumatic history.",
3:"Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy gives a dynamic outflow murmur with a thick septum and a normal valve. This valve is calcified and the septum is normal.",
4:"Syphilitic aortitis dilates the aortic root and causes aortic <b>regurgitation</b> (a diastolic murmur), not a calcified, stenotic valve."},
eli5="He was born with an aortic valve that has two flaps instead of three. Two flaps take more wear and tear, so they harden with calcium much earlier in life. By his 50s the valve barely opens, so he gets chest pain and faints when he exerts himself."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 26-year-old woman comes to the office because of intermittent palpitations for 6 months. She has no history of serious illness. Her pulse is 78/min and regular, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. She is 165 cm (5 ft 5 in) tall, with normal arm span and no lens abnormalities. A midsystolic click followed by a late systolic murmur is heard at the apex. When she stands up, the click moves earlier in systole. Echocardiography shows billowing of both mitral leaflets into the left atrium during systole.{FIG}Histologic examination of this patient's mitral leaflets would most likely show which of the following?",
choices=["Aschoff bodies with Anitschkow cells","Dystrophic calcification of the annulus","Fibrosis with fused commissures and new vessels","Myxoid tissue with excess proteoglycans","Sterile platelet–fibrin thrombi on the surface"], key=3,
exp="A midsystolic click and late systolic murmur that move earlier on standing (less left ventricular volume → the leaflets prolapse sooner), with leaflets billowing into the atrium, is <b>mitral valve prolapse</b>.<br><br>The lecture's term is <b>myxomatous degeneration</b>: the middle (spongiosa) layer of the leaflet thickens with <b>proteoglycan-rich myxoid tissue</b>, and the collagen and elastic layers weaken → floppy, redundant leaflets and elongated chordae → prolapse → mitral regurgitation. It is common in young women, of unclear cause (also seen in Marfan syndrome), and is listed as an uncommon cause of <b>sudden death</b> in young women. With regurgitation, it is now the commonest predisposing lesion for endocarditis in the United States.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Mitral valve prolapse is caused by myxomatous degeneration: proteoglycan-rich myxoid expansion of the leaflets, making them floppy and redundant.",
wrong={0:"Aschoff bodies are the lesion of <b>acute rheumatic fever</b>, found in a child with migratory arthritis and carditis after a strep throat.",
1:"Calcification of the mitral annulus is an age-related change in older adults. It causes regurgitation or conduction problems, not billowing leaflets in a 26-year-old.",
2:"Fibrosis with fused commissures and neovascularization is <b>chronic rheumatic</b> disease, which produces stenosis with an opening snap, not a click.",
4:"Sterile platelet–fibrin thrombi are nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis in hypercoagulable states such as cancer."},
eli5="The flaps of her mitral valve are made of floppy, jelly-like tissue instead of tough fibre. When the heart squeezes, they bulge back like a sail, which makes a click. Sometimes they leak a little, making a murmur."),

dict(img='iron', cap='Endomyocardial biopsy, Prussian blue stain',
stem="A 52-year-old man comes to the office because of shortness of breath, fatigue, and joint pain in his hands for 1 year. He was diagnosed with diabetes mellitus 2 years ago. He drinks 1 beer per week and has never received a blood transfusion. His skin has a gray-bronze color. The liver is palpable 4 cm below the costal margin. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30%. Coronary angiography shows no stenosis. An endomyocardial biopsy specimen is shown.{FIG}A mutation in which of the following genes is most likely responsible for this patient's cardiac condition?",
choices=["ATP7B","HFE","MYH7","SERPINA1","TTR"], key=1,
exp="Prussian blue stains abundant <b>iron</b> (blue granules) inside the cardiac myocytes. Bronze skin + diabetes (\"bronze diabetes\") + hepatomegaly + arthropathy + cardiomyopathy in a middle-aged man without transfusions = <b>hereditary hemochromatosis</b>.<br><br>An <b>HFE</b> mutation (usually C282Y, autosomal recessive) → low hepcidin → uncontrolled intestinal iron absorption → iron deposits in liver, pancreas, skin, joints and heart → free-radical injury to myocytes → a <b>dilated</b> (sometimes restrictive) cardiomyopathy. The lecture lists hemochromatosis among causes of dilated cardiomyopathy. Labs show high ferritin and transferrin saturation. Phlebotomy can reverse the cardiac dysfunction if started early.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hereditary hemochromatosis (HFE mutation) causes iron deposition in myocytes (Prussian blue positive) and a dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy, with bronze skin, diabetes and liver disease.",
wrong={0:"ATP7B mutations cause <b>Wilson disease</b> (copper accumulation): liver disease, neuropsychiatric symptoms and Kayser–Fleischer rings, not iron in myocytes.",
2:"MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain) mutations cause <b>hypertrophic</b> cardiomyopathy with myofibre disarray, not iron deposition or bronze diabetes.",
3:"SERPINA1 mutations cause alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, with emphysema and liver disease, and do not cause cardiomyopathy or iron deposition.",
4:"TTR (transthyretin) mutations cause hereditary <b>amyloid</b> cardiomyopathy, which is Congo red–positive and restrictive, not Prussian blue–positive."},
eli5="His gut soaks up far too much iron from food because of an inherited gene change. The extra iron piles up in his heart, liver, pancreas and skin. It poisons the heart muscle so it stretches and weakens, and the blue stain shows the iron."),

dict(img='brugada', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 41-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after his wife was awakened at night by his gasping, labored breathing; he was briefly unresponsive and then recovered. He has no history of serious illness and takes no medications. His older brother died suddenly in his sleep at age 38 years. Vital signs and physical examination show no abnormalities. Serum electrolyte concentrations are within the reference ranges. Echocardiography shows a structurally normal heart. An ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's condition?",
choices=["Accessory atrioventricular conduction pathway","Gain of function of a cardiac sodium channel","Gain of function of a ryanodine receptor","Loss of function of a cardiac potassium channel","Loss of function of a cardiac sodium channel"], key=4,
exp="The ECG shows <b>coved ST elevation in V1–V2</b> with a right bundle branch block–like pattern, the <b>Brugada pattern</b>. With a nocturnal arrhythmic event (agonal breathing during sleep), a sibling's sudden death in his sleep, and a structurally normal heart, this is <b>Brugada syndrome</b>.<br><br>Most commonly, <b>loss of function of the cardiac sodium channel SCN5A</b> (also CACNB2b, a calcium channel, and SCN1b) → an imbalance of currents in the right ventricular outflow epicardium → ST elevation in V1–V3 and a substrate for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, typically at rest or during sleep. Treatment is an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator. The lecture's rule: same gene, opposite direction, opposite disease. SCN5A <b>gain</b> of function gives long QT (type 3), and SCN5A <b>loss</b> gives Brugada.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Brugada syndrome (coved ST elevation in V1–V3, sudden death during sleep) is usually caused by loss of function of the cardiac sodium channel SCN5A.",
wrong={0:"An accessory pathway (Wolff–Parkinson–White) gives a short PR and a delta wave, not coved ST elevation in V1–V2.",
1:"SCN5A <b>gain</b> of function prolongs the QT (long QT type 3). Here the QT is not prolonged and the ST segments are coved in V1–V2.",
3:"Potassium channel loss of function (KCNQ1, KCNH2) causes long QT syndrome with events triggered by exertion or startle. The ECG would show a long QT.",
2:"Ryanodine receptor gain of function causes catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, triggered by exercise or emotion, with a normal resting ECG."},
eli5="A faulty sodium doorway in his heart cells makes the right side of the heart reset unevenly. That shows up as a strange hump on the first chest leads of the ECG. During sleep it can set off a deadly rhythm, which is probably what happened to his brother."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 45-year-old woman comes to the office because of progressive shortness of breath, leg swelling, and abdominal distention for 1 year. At age 20 years, she was treated for Hodgkin lymphoma with chemotherapy and mediastinal radiation. Her pulse is 102/min and blood pressure is 106/74 mm Hg. Jugular venous pressure is elevated and rises further with inspiration. A high-pitched sound is heard in early diastole. The lungs are clear. There is shifting dullness of the abdomen and 2+ pitting edema of the legs. Echocardiography shows normal ventricular wall thickness and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 60%. CT scan of the chest shows a thickened, calcified pericardium without effusion.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Anthracycline-induced dilated cardiomyopathy","Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis","Cor pulmonale","Radiation-induced restrictive cardiomyopathy"], key=2,
exp="Right-sided congestion (raised neck veins, ascites, edema) with clear lungs, a <b>Kussmaul sign</b> (neck veins rise with inspiration), an early diastolic <b>pericardial knock</b>, normal ventricular walls and function, and a <b>thickened, calcified pericardium</b> = <b>constrictive pericarditis</b>.<br><br>Mediastinal radiation → the pericardium becomes fibrotic over years to decades → a rigid shell → the ventricles fill briskly in early diastole and then stop abruptly (the knock) → systemic venous congestion. The lecture lists radiation among the iatrogenic causes of fibrosis: <b>restrictive cardiomyopathy, constrictive pericarditis and accelerated coronary disease</b>. Constriction is the classic mimic of restrictive cardiomyopathy. The thick pericardium on imaging is what separates them, and constriction can be cured by pericardiectomy.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Constrictive pericarditis (Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock, thick or calcified pericardium, normal ventricles) is a late complication of mediastinal radiation and is distinguished from restrictive cardiomyopathy by the thickened pericardium.",
wrong={0:"Hodgkin chemotherapy includes doxorubicin, but anthracycline cardiomyopathy gives a <b>dilated</b> ventricle with a low ejection fraction. Hers is 60%.",
1:"Tamponade needs a pericardial <b>effusion</b>, and the CT shows none. Kussmaul sign is typically absent in tamponade, where pulsus paradoxus is the key sign.",
3:"Cor pulmonale requires lung disease raising pulmonary pressures, and it causes right ventricular hypertrophy. It does not thicken or calcify the pericardium.",
4:"Radiation can also stiffen the myocardium, but restrictive cardiomyopathy shows a <b>normal</b> pericardium, often with thick walls or big atria. Here the thick, calcified pericardium points to constriction."},
eli5="Radiation to her chest decades ago slowly turned the sac around her heart into a hard, chalky shell. The heart can't expand to fill, so blood backs up into her neck veins, belly and legs. Peeling off the shell with surgery can fix it."),

dict(img='libman', cap='Mitral valve at autopsy (arrowhead marks a lesion)',
stem="A 34-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has had 2 second-trimester pregnancy losses. Her activated partial thromboplastin time is prolonged and does not correct when her plasma is mixed with normal plasma. She has had no fevers, and she has no known cancer. She is admitted with sudden right-sided weakness and dies 5 days later. Blood cultures before her death showed no growth, and she had received no antibiotics. At autopsy, the mitral valve is shown. The small, flat vegetations lie on both the atrial and ventricular surfaces of the leaflets and on the chordae, and the leaflets are not perforated. No tumor is found.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Acute rheumatic valvulitis","Libman–Sacks endocarditis","Marantic endocarditis of malignancy","Myxomatous mitral valve degeneration","Subacute infective endocarditis"], key=1,
exp="Small, flat, sterile vegetations on <b>both surfaces</b> of the mitral leaflets and on the chordae in a woman with lupus and <b>antiphospholipid antibodies</b> (a lupus anticoagulant that prolongs the aPTT and does not correct on mixing, plus recurrent pregnancy loss) = <b>Libman–Sacks endocarditis</b>.<br><br>Lupus and antiphospholipid syndrome → endothelial injury and a prothrombotic state → sterile fibrin–platelet deposits, with immune complexes and inflammation, anywhere on the leaflet → these can embolize (stroke) and scar the valve (regurgitation). The lecture lists non-infective vegetations in lupus (Libman–Sacks) and in malignancy (marantic).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Libman–Sacks endocarditis is sterile, small, flat vegetations on both sides of the mitral leaflets in systemic lupus erythematosus, especially with antiphospholipid antibodies, and it can cause embolic stroke.",
wrong={0:"Acute rheumatic valvulitis gives small warty vegetations along the closure lines after a group A strep infection in a child, with Aschoff bodies. There is no rheumatic history.",
2:"Marantic endocarditis is the same kind of sterile vegetation, but by definition it occurs with <b>malignancy</b>, and no tumor was found.",
3:"Myxomatous degeneration makes floppy, redundant leaflets (prolapse) without vegetations.",
4:"Infective endocarditis would cause fever, positive blood cultures and destructive vegetations. She had no fevers and her cultures were sterile without prior antibiotics."},
eli5="In lupus with 'sticky blood' antibodies, small sterile clumps form on both sides of the heart valve flaps. No germs are involved. A piece broke off and blocked an artery in her brain."),
]
